\layout Question

In an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue inter-arrival times are exponentially distributed and service times
 are `generally' distributed.
 The average wait in the queue may be derived in terms of the residual service
 time as follows
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho},\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset 

 is the average residual service time and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

 is the queue utilization factor.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Qupart

Describe, using diagrams where necessary, the concept of residual service
 time.
 State the assumptions used when deriving the formula above.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{qup:1a}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

30
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The residual service time is the remaining service time as perceived by
 an arriving customer.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th customer arrives and waits 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{i}$
\end_inset 

 time units for the current customer under service to finish being served.
 This is the residual service time.
 The diagram below explains this concept:
\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/residualServiceOverview.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the diagram the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arriving packet perceives a queueing time of 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}$
\end_inset 

 and a residual service time of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{i}$
\end_inset 

.
 The average residual service time is the average 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{i}$
\end_inset 

 for all packets.
 
\layout Standard

The assumptions used in deriving the formula are
\layout Itemize

We assume that time averages are equivalent to steady state averages.
\layout Itemize

We assume that service times have a finite variance.
\layout Itemize

We assume that the arrival times are statistically independent of the service
 times.
\layout Itemize

Assume the queue is FIFO.
\layout Standard


\series bold 
\emph on 
5 marks for each assumption, 10 marks for the description of residual service
 time+diagram
\series default 
\emph default 
.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\layout Qupart

Use a graphical argument to derive a formula for the residual service time.
 Combine this equation with the formula in part a) for the average waiting
 time to show that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2\left(1-\rho\right)},\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is the arrival rate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 is the second moment of the service time distribution.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

40
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

We again turn to a graphical proof to obtain the value of the mean residual
 service time.
 A plot can be drawn of the residual service time over time.
\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/residualTime.eps
	width 70text%

\end_inset 

 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 is the number of service completions in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,\, t]$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

The time average of the mean residual service time can then be calculated
 in terms of the area under the curve, 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

, which is given by the area under each of the triangles,
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
A(t)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}.\]

\end_inset 

 The average service time is the area divided by time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{A(t)}{t} & = & \frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{A(t)}{M(t)}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{2}\frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}}{M(t)}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

where we have used the number of arrivals after time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, as 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, 
\emph on 
if the variance of the service time distribution is finite
\emph default 
, we find that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle .\]

\end_inset 

 Again, assuming that time averages and ensemble averages are equal, we
 substitute back into the equation provided in the previous part to obtain
 the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho)}.\]

\end_inset 


\series bold 
\emph on 
15 for the diagram, 10 for the area formula, 10 for the introduction of
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 and 5 for the right answer.

\series default 
\emph default 
 
\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\layout Qupart

The equation you have derived is recognized as the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula.
 Use it to derive the average waiting time in an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

15
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

In an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue there is an exponential service time,
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(X\right)=\mu\exp\left(-\mu x\right).\]

\end_inset 

The mean of this service time is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\textrm{var}\left(X\right)=\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}$
\end_inset 

.
 Therefore the second moment is therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\left\langle X\right\rangle ^{2}+\textrm{var}\left(X\right)=\frac{2}{\mu^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

Substituting into the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu^{2}\left(1-\rho\right)}=\frac{\rho}{\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}\]

\end_inset 


\series bold 
\emph on 
5 marks for the distribution, 5 for the second moment and 5 marks for the
 result
\series default 
\emph default 

