status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\layout Qupart

Which service time distribution leads to the lowest delay for a given mean?
 What characteristic of this distribution ensures it has the lowest associated
 delay?
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

15
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The service time distribution that leads to the lowest delay for a given
 mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 is the deterministic service time, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/D/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 This is because it is the distribution with the lowest variance (the variance
 is zero) and therefore the second moment, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\bar{X}^{2}+\textrm{var}\left(X\right)$
\end_inset 

 is as low as it can be for this distribution (the variance cannot be negative)
 which in turn means the average delay is as low as it can be.
 There is no other distribution with a variance of zero.
 
\layout Standard


\series bold 
\emph on 
5 marks for the right answer, and 10 marks for the fact that it is the lowest
 variance
\series default 
\emph default 
.
