\layout Qupart

The steady state distribution for the Erlang loss system is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{k}}{k!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}.\]

\end_inset 

If the number of servers is very large (
\begin_inset Formula $m>>a)$
\end_inset 

 what will the average number of customers in the system be?
\layout Qupart


\emph on 
Hint: consider what happens in the system as 
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow\infty.$
\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

25
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

As the question states we should consider the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 The steady state distribution for Erlang Loss is a truncated Poisson.
 In the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 (which is an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue), the truncated Poisson becomes a Poisson distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}=\exp\left(a\right),\]

\end_inset 

so we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\exp\left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the mean of this distribution is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{a}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\series bold 
\emph on 
15 for determining that the system's steady state approximates a Poisson
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $m>>a$
\end_inset 

 (either by stating its originally a truncated Poisson, or by producing
 the result for the infinte sum becoming an exponential.) 10 for knowing
 (or proving!) that the mean of a Poisson is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{a}$
\end_inset 


\series default 
\emph default 
.
