\layout Qupart

The Erlang B formula is associated with the Erlang loss queueing system.
 Under what conditions does the steady state distribution exist for this
 queueing system? What is the form of this steady state distribution when
 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 becomes much larger than 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 and what is the resulting effect on the carried load?
\layout Mypercent

30
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer 
\layout Standard

The steady state 
\emph on 
always exists
\emph default 
.
 In some systems steady state doesn't exist when the offered load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

, is higher than the number of servers, 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

, however in the Erlang loss system the steady state will converge towards
 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)=1$
\end_inset 

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=m$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)=0$
\end_inset 

 otherwise.
 In this case most packets are rejected and the carried load becomes very
 low.
 The steady state exists because the system is lossy.
\layout Standard


