\layout Qupart

Packet arrivals occur as a Poisson distribution at 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{node1}

\end_inset 

 with a rate of 2 per second.
 Packet lengths are distributed as an exponential distribution with a mean
 of 0.5 Kb.
 Assuming that packets are sent randomly along all possible routes with
 equal probability, use Kleinrock's independence assumption to estimate
 the steady state average number of packets at each link and the average
 delay for each route.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

25
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

Packets arrive as a Poisson with a rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

.
 They are sent randomly along the two routes with equal probaility.
 This means, via the decomposition property of Poisson processes, that the
 rate for each route is 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2\times0.5=1$
\end_inset 

.
 The service time distribution is dicated by the length of the packet and
 the bandwidth of the route.
 The bandwidth (given in the code above) is 2Kb/s.
 Packet lengths are 0.5 Kb, so the service time distribution for all links
 will have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{2}{0.4}=4$
\end_inset 

 packets/s.
 Kleinrock's independence assumption tells us to treat each queue independently.
 The first route consists of two queues.
 
\layout Standard

The delay is given by the formula for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue which is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda}=\frac{1}{4-1}=\frac{1}{3}s.\]

\end_inset 

There are two queues in the first route so the total delay is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{2}{3}s$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

The calculation is the same for the second route, but there there is only
 one queue (so in fact you don't need Kleinrock's assumption --- see later
 question part) and the delay in the second route is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{3}s$
\end_inset 

.
 
\series bold 
\emph on 
5 for decomposition of Poissons 10 for the correct formula and 5 for the
 correct answer for each route.
\series default 
\emph default 

