One of the routes in the network contains two queues.
 Explain how the second queue's size, as perceived by an arriving packet,
 will be related to the arriving packet's length.
 How will this waiting time be affected for longer packets arriving at the
 second queue? 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

20
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The longer packets will arrive at the second queue to find a shorter queue
 than the shorter packets.
 Or in other words queue length at the second queue are anti correlated
 with packet lengths.
 This is because, while the longer packet was being served at the first
 queue there is time for the second queue to empty out.
 This is analogous to a slow moving truck driver who will generally see
 an open road in front of him.
\layout Standard


\series bold 
\emph on 
10 marks for correctly saying that the queue will be shorter for longer
 packets (or vice versa).
 10 Marks for saying the longer packet will wait less time at the second
 queue.

