Typically, in a large network, analytical modelling of the system will not
 be possible.
 In practice we often turn to simulation for modelling of such systems.
 Why might the sample-based approximations to the system's characteristics
 we obtain from these simulations be misleading?
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

10
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

If the simulation is not run long enough it may not reach it's steady state.
 It may even be hard to determine whether the system has a steady state.
 If samples from the system are taken too close together in time then they
 are not truly independent and sample based estimates will be incorrect.
 
